 # Compositing and Output Generation
-
-
 ## Overview
-Compositing and Output Generation defines the final stage of direct volume ray casting, where discrete volumetric samples are aggregated into a 2D image pixel [G]. It solves the problem of optical integration, determining how light emitted or scattered by volume elements is attenuated by absorption and combined with background light [G]. This family builds on [Multi-Pass and Compositing Techniques](14748-multi-pass-and-compositing-techniques.md) and is distinct from [Projection and MIP Techniques](6305-projection-and-mip-techniques.md) because it explicitly tracks opacity and transmittance for alpha blending, preserving depth cues, whereas MIP methods discard opacity for order-independent extremum aggregation. It differs from [Volume Raycasting Output Generation](4277-volume-raycasting-output-generation.md) by focusing on the mathematical accumulation logic itself, while the sibling article covers the broader implementation pipeline including ray traversal and transfer function sampling.
